"""Original practice content. Run to reproduce the public content JSON."""
import json, math
from pathlib import Path
Q=[]
def q(id,title,topic,level,path,minutes,prompt,answer,hints,solution,follow,follow_answer,lesson,tol=1e-6):
    Q.append(dict(id=id,title=title,topic=topic,difficulty=level,path=path,minutes=minutes,prompt=prompt,answer=answer,hints=hints,solution=solution,followup=follow,followSolution=follow_answer,lesson=lesson,tolerance=tol,type='numeric' if answer is not None else 'written',rubric=['State the assumptions and the object you are computing.','Show the decisive derivation or counterexample.','Explain why the tempting shortcut fails.']))
q('q01','One six is enough','Probability','Easy','shared',3,'Roll two independent fair six-sided dice. What is the probability that at least one is a six?',11/36,['Count the complement.','Neither die is a six with probability (5/6)².'],'The complement has probability 25/36. Subtract it from 1 to obtain 11/36. Adding 1/6 + 1/6 counts the double-six outcome twice.','Now roll n dice. How many are needed to make the probability exceed 90%?','Require (5/6)^n < 0.1. Taking logs gives n > 12.63, so 13 dice.','l01')
q('q02','A sum changes the sample space','Conditioning','Easy','shared',4,'Two fair dice have sum 8. Given this information, what is the probability that their values are equal?',1/5,['List ordered pairs with sum 8.','There are five possible ordered pairs.'],'The conditional sample space is (2,6), (3,5), (4,4), (5,3), (6,2). Only (4,4) is a double, so the answer is 1/5. Independence before conditioning does not imply independence after it.','What is the conditional mean of the first die?','By symmetry the two conditional means are equal and sum to 8, so each is 4.','l02')
q('q03','Learn which coin you picked','Bayes','Medium','shared',6,'Choose with equal probability between a fair coin and a coin that always lands heads. You flip the chosen coin once and see heads. What is the probability it is the always-heads coin?',2/3,['Write the likelihood under each coin.','The joint probabilities of selection and a head are 1/2 and 1/4.'],'The always-heads branch contributes 1/2 to the probability of heads. The fair branch contributes 1/4. Normalising gives (1/2)/(3/4)=2/3. The posterior is not the prior 1/2.','After k consecutive heads, derive the posterior.','It is 1/(1+2^(-k)). One tail rules out the always-heads coin entirely.','l02')
q('q04','A promising signal with a low base rate','Bayes','Medium','researcher',7,'Only 2% of candidate signals are genuinely useful. A test passes a useful signal with probability 90% and a useless one with probability 10%. Given a pass, what is the probability the signal is useful?',9/58,['Use base rates as well as test accuracy.','The two pass masses are 0.02×0.9 and 0.98×0.1.'],'P(useful | pass)=0.018/(0.018+0.098)=9/58≈0.15517. High sensitivity does not compensate for many false passes from the large useless population. This calculation assumes the stated base rate and test characteristics apply to the candidate pool.','If you independently run the test twice and both pass, what changes?','Under conditional independence given usefulness, the posterior is 0.02×0.9² / (0.02×0.9²+0.98×0.1²)=81/130≈0.62308. Independence is a substantive assumption.','l15')
q('q05','Without replacement','Counting','Easy','shared',4,'An urn contains 6 red and 4 blue balls. Draw two uniformly without replacement. What is the probability that their colours differ?',8/15,['Count red-blue unordered pairs.','There are 6×4 favourable pairs and C(10,2) total pairs.'],'There are 24 mixed-colour pairs out of 45 possible pairs, giving 8/15. Alternatively sum (6/10)(4/9)+(4/10)(6/9). Replacement would change the second denominator.','Generalise to r red and b blue.','The probability is 2rb/[(r+b)(r+b-1)].','l01')
q('q06','Overlapping heads','Expectation','Medium','shared',6,'Flip a fair coin 20 times. Count adjacent HH pairs, allowing overlaps (HHH contains two pairs). What is the expected count?',19/4,['Create an indicator for each adjacent position.','Independence between the indicators is unnecessary for the mean.'],'There are 19 adjacent positions. Each indicator has expectation 1/4, so linearity gives 19/4=4.75. The indicators overlap, but linearity of expectation still holds.','For n flips, derive the variance of the count.','For n≥2, each indicator has variance 3/16; adjacent indicators have covariance 1/8−1/16=1/16 and disjoint ones have covariance zero. The variance is (3(n−1)+2(n−2))/16=(5n−7)/16.','l03')
q('q07','The larger die','Expectation','Medium','shared',6,'Roll two independent fair six-sided dice. What is the expected maximum?',161/36,['Use P(max=k), or a tail sum.','P(max≤k)=k²/36.'],'P(max=k)=(k²−(k−1)²)/36=(2k−1)/36. Sum k(2k−1)/36 from 1 to 6 to get 161/36≈4.47222. A maximum is not the maximum of the means.','What is the expected minimum?','max+min equals the sum pointwise. Thus E[min]=7−161/36=91/36.','l03')
q('q08','The first uniform point','Distributions','Medium','shared',6,'Draw three independent Uniform(0,1) values. What is the expected minimum?',1/4,['Integrate the survival function.','P(min>x)=(1−x)³ for 0≤x≤1.'],'For a nonnegative variable, E[M]=∫P(M>x)dx. Here ∫₀¹(1−x)³dx=1/4. In general the minimum of n independent Uniform(0,1) values has mean 1/(n+1).','Find E[max] and its CDF.','P(max≤x)=x³ on [0,1], and E[max]=3/4.','l04')
q('q09','Collect all four','Stopping times','Medium','shared',7,'Each independent draw returns one of four equally likely symbols. How many draws do you expect before seeing all four?',25/3,['Split the wait into stages after each new symbol.','The success probabilities are 1, 3/4, 1/2, 1/4.'],'The expected stage lengths are 1, 4/3, 2, 4. Their sum is 25/3. Geometric waiting times need not be identically distributed for their expectations to add.','Generalise to m equally likely symbols.','The mean is m(1+1/2+…+1/m). Unequal probabilities require a different argument; replacing them by their average is invalid.','l05')
q('q10','Wait for a head then a tail','Stopping times','Medium','shared',7,'Flip a fair coin until the consecutive pattern HT appears. What is the expected number of flips?',4,['Track whether the latest flip was H.','Write one recurrence from the empty state and one from H.'],'Let E₀ be the wait from no useful suffix and E_H from suffix H. E₀=1+(E₀+E_H)/2 and E_H=1+E_H/2. Thus E_H=2 and E₀=4. Repeated H leaves you in the H state.','Why does HH have a different wait?','For HH, E_H=1+E₀/2. Solve with the same E₀ equation to get E₀=6. A tail destroys the partial match.','l05')
q('q11','Three heads in a row','Stopping times','Hard','shared',12,'Flip a fair coin until HHH first appears. Find the expected total number of flips, including the final three.',14,['Use states with 0, 1 and 2 trailing heads.','A tail sends every nonterminal state to state 0.','E₂=1+E₀/2; E₁=1+(E₂+E₀)/2; E₀=1+(E₁+E₀)/2.'],'The stated recurrences give E₁=E₀−2 and E₂=1+E₀/2. Substitution yields E₀−2=1+(1+1.5E₀)/2, so E₀=14. The shortcut 1/P(HHH)=8 treats overlapping trials as disjoint independent attempts.','Generalise to k heads with head probability p.','The expected wait is (1−p^k)/[(1−p)p^k] for 0<p<1. At p=1 it is k by continuity.','l05')
q('q12','Exit a fair random walk','Stopping times','Hard','shared',12,'A simple symmetric random walk starts at 5 on the integers and stops on first hitting 0 or 12. What is its expected stopping time?',35,['The mean solves t_i=1+(t_(i−1)+t_(i+1))/2.','Use boundary conditions t_0=t_12=0.','Try a quadratic in i.'],'The solution is t_i=i(12−i): its second discrete difference is −2 and it is zero at the boundaries. Hence t_5=35. The finite interval ensures finite expected exit time.','What is the probability of hitting 12 first?','Solve p_i=(p_(i−1)+p_(i+1))/2 with p_0=0,p_12=1. The answer is i/12, hence 5/12.','l05')
q('q13','One reroll','Decision theory','Medium','trader',7,'Roll a fair die and observe it. Either keep the value or discard it and roll once more, whose value you must accept. What is the optimal expected payoff?',4.25,['Compare each observed value with the mean of a fresh roll.','Keep 4, 5, 6; reroll 1, 2, 3.'],'The continuation value is 3.5. The expected optimum is (3×3.5+4+5+6)/6=4.25. The realised second roll can be worse, but the decision is based on conditional expectation.','If a reroll costs 1, what is the optimal value?','Continuation is 2.5. Reroll 1 and 2, keep 3 through 6. Mean=(2×2.5+3+4+5+6)/6=23/6.','l21')
q('q14','Three pieces, one triangle','Geometry','Hard','shared',12,'Choose two independent Uniform(0,1) cut locations on a unit stick. Cut at both. What is the probability the three lengths form a nondegenerate triangle?',0.25,['A triangle exists iff no piece is at least half the stick.','Order the cuts U<V; the three pieces are U,V−U,1−V.','Measure the valid region inside 0<U<V<1.'],'The valid conditions are U<1/2, V>1/2 and V−U<1/2. Their region is a triangle of area 1/8 inside the ordered-cut triangle of area 1/2. The ratio is 1/4. Equality boundaries have probability zero.','Does sequentially choosing a piece uniformly and cutting it uniformly give the same answer?','No. It defines a different distribution over length triples. Specify the cutting procedure before using a geometric uniformity argument.','l04')
q('q15','A memoryless wait','Distributions','Easy','shared',4,'A waiting time is Exponential with rate 1/2 per minute. Given that you have already waited 3 minutes, what is the expected total waiting time in minutes?',5,['Distinguish total time from remaining time.','The expected remaining time is 1/rate.'],'Memorylessness gives E[T−3 | T>3]=2. The expected total is 3+2=5. Memorylessness is specific to this model; an arbitrary wait distribution does not have it.','What is P(T>7 | T>3)?','It is exp(−(1/2)×4)=exp(−2).','l04')
q('q16','Which clock rings first?','Distributions','Medium','shared',6,'Independent exponential clocks have rates 3 and 2. What is the probability that the rate-3 clock rings first?',0.6,['Integrate f_X(t)P(Y>t).','The integrand is 3 exp(−5t).'],'∫₀∞3e^(−3t)e^(−2t)dt=3/5. The minimum has rate 5. Ties have probability zero.','Are the winning clock and the time of the first ring independent?','Yes for independent exponential clocks with constant rates. The joint density of winner 1 and time t is 3e^(−5t), which factors into 3/5 times 5e^(−5t).','l04')
q('q17','Correlation survives averaging','Statistics','Medium','researcher',8,'One hundred variables each have variance 1. Every distinct pair has correlation 0.1. What is the variance of their arithmetic mean?',0.109,['Expand the variance of a sum, including off-diagonal terms.','There are n(n−1) ordered covariance terms.'],'Var(mean)=[n+n(n−1)ρ]/n²=ρ+(1−ρ)/n=0.109. The independence shortcut gives 0.01 and misses the common component.','What happens as n grows?','The variance tends to ρ=0.1; averaging does not eliminate the common shock. Equicorrelation must also satisfy ρ≥−1/(n−1).','l07')
q('q18','A hundred dependent observations','Time series','Hard','researcher',12,'A stationary AR(1) series has lag-one autocorrelation ρ=0.8 and marginal variance σ². Define effective sample size by equating the mean-estimation variance to σ²/n_eff. Derive the large-sample approximation, then report n_eff for n=100. Use the asymptotic result, not the finite-n tapered correction.',100/9,['The autocorrelation at lag k is ρ^k.','The variance inflation factor is 1+2Σρ^k=(1+ρ)/(1−ρ).'],'The approximation gives 100×0.2/1.8=100/9≈11.11. This is an asymptotic mean-estimation comparison, not a universal effective sample size for every estimator.','Write the exact finite-n variance factor.','Var(mean)/[γ₀/n]=1+2Σ_(k=1)^(n−1)(1−k/n)ρ^k. The taper matters in short samples or near unit roots.','l13')
q('q19','Measurement error shrinks a slope','Regression','Hard','researcher',12,'Let Y=2X+ε. You observe W=X+u. X,u,ε are mutually independent, centred, with Var(X)=1 and Var(u)=3. What is the population OLS slope of Y on W with an intercept?',0.5,['The slope is Cov(W,Y)/Var(W).','The numerator is 2 and denominator 4.'],'Cov(W,Y)=2Var(X)=2 and Var(W)=1+3=4, hence slope 1/2. Independent measurement error in the regressor attenuates the slope; error in the response alone would not.','What if u is correlated with ε?','The numerator gains Cov(u,ε). The attenuation conclusion can fail, including sign reversal. Independence was doing real work.','l11')
q('q20','Three correlations must fit together','Linear algebra','Hard','researcher',14,'Standardised variables X,Y,Z satisfy Corr(X,Y)=Corr(Y,Z)=0.8. What is the smallest possible Corr(X,Z)?',0.28,['The correlation matrix must be positive semidefinite.','Let a=Corr(X,Y), b=Corr(Y,Z), c=Corr(X,Z).','The determinant gives (c−ab)²≤(1−a²)(1−b²).'],'The allowed interval is ab±√[(1−a²)(1−b²)]. Here it is 0.64±0.36, so c≥0.28. Pairwise bounds [−1,1] alone are insufficient. At the boundary the matrix is singular, and the bound is attainable.','Construct variables attaining the minimum.','Take independent standardised Y,U and set X=0.8Y+0.6U, Z=0.8Y−0.6U. Then Corr(X,Z)=0.64−0.36=0.28.','l10')
q('q21','Residuals sum to zero','Regression','Medium','researcher',8,'You fit OLS with an intercept. Explain why the residuals sum to zero, and why this need not hold without an intercept.',None,['Write the derivative of squared error with respect to the intercept.','Equivalently use Xᵀr=0.'],'At a least-squares optimum, differentiating Σ(y_i−a−bᵀx_i)² with respect to a gives −2Σr_i=0. In matrix terms, residuals are orthogonal to every design column, including the all-ones intercept column. Without that column there is no such normal equation.','Does zero residual mean imply the model is unbiased out of sample?','No. It is an in-sample algebraic property. Misspecification, leakage or a changed distribution can produce arbitrarily biased future predictions.','l11')
q('q22','A reversal after pooling','Statistics','Hard','researcher',12,'Can a treatment have a higher success rate in each of two groups but a lower success rate overall? Construct a numerical example and explain the mechanism.',None,['Use different group weights for treatment and control.','Place most treatment observations in the harder group.'],'Example: easy group treatment 9/10 versus control 80/100; hard group treatment 20/100 versus control 1/10. Treatment wins within each (90%>80%,20%>10%), but overall treatment is 29/110≈26.4% and control 81/110≈73.6%. Group composition confounds the pooled comparison.','Does adjusting for every available variable fix causal inference?','No. Conditioning on colliders can introduce bias, and unmeasured confounding remains possible. Adjustment needs a causal argument.','l12')
q('q23','Estimate the end of a uniform interval','Estimation','Medium','researcher',9,'You observe n=4 iid Uniform(0,θ) values. The sample maximum is 8. Multiply it by the appropriate factor to obtain an unbiased estimator of θ. What estimate do you get?',10,['E[max]=nθ/(n+1).','Multiply by (n+1)/n.'],'The maximum has CDF (m/θ)^n on [0,θ] and mean nθ/(n+1). The unbiased estimator is (n+1)max/n. At n=4,max=8 it equals 10. The maximum itself is the MLE but is biased down.','Is unbiasedness alone enough to choose an estimator?','No. Compare mean squared error, variance, stability and loss function. An unbiased estimator can have higher risk than a biased one.','l09')
q('q24','The unbiased variance divisor','Estimation','Medium','researcher',8,'For iid observations with finite variance σ², explain why Σ(X_i−X̄)²/n is biased down, and derive the unbiased correction.',None,['Use Σ(X_i−X̄)²=Σ(X_i−μ)²−n(X̄−μ)².','Take expectations and use Var(X̄)=σ²/n.'],'Use Σ(X_i−X̄)²=Σ(X_i−μ)²−n(X̄−μ)². The expectation of the first term is nσ² and that of the subtracted term is n Var(X̄)=σ², so the sum has expectation (n−1)σ². Dividing by n−1 gives an unbiased estimator for n>1. Normality is not needed for this expectation identity.','What breaks for correlated data?','Var(X̄) is no longer σ²/n. The same identity holds, but the familiar divisor does not generally remove the bias.','l09')
q('q25','At least one false discovery','Validation','Medium','researcher',8,'You run 20 independent tests of true null hypotheses, each at level 5%. What is the probability of at least one rejection?',1-.95**20,['Work with the complement.','Independence is explicitly assumed.'],'The probability of no rejection is 0.95²⁰. The complement is about 0.641514. A single-test error rate is not a familywise error rate. Dependence changes the exact calculation.','Give a threshold that controls familywise error at 5% without independence.','Bonferroni uses 0.05/20=0.0025 per test. The union bound gives control for arbitrary dependence, assuming valid individual p-values.','l15')
q('q26','A p-value is not a posterior','Inference','Medium','researcher',8,'A paper reports p=0.03. Is there a 97% probability its hypothesis is true? Define the p-value precisely and identify the missing ingredients for a posterior probability.',None,['The conditioning is on the null model, not the observed data.','A posterior needs a prior and an alternative likelihood.'],'A p-value is the probability, under the specified null and test procedure, of a statistic at least as extreme as observed. It is not P(null | data). A posterior needs prior model probabilities and likelihoods under null and alternatives. Selection and multiple tests also affect interpretation.','Does a large p-value establish the null?','No. The test may have low power. Equivalence or noninferiority questions need their own margins and tests.','l12')
q('q27','Random folds on overlapping labels','Validation','Hard','researcher',14,'Every sample at time t predicts the return from t to t+5. A researcher randomly splits rows into train and test and gets excellent results. Diagnose at least three problems and design a replacement validation scheme.',None,['Think label intervals, temporal dependence and preprocessing.','A training label may include outcomes inside a test interval.','Separate model selection from the final performance estimate.'],'Random splitting can put overlapping label outcomes in both sets; it can train on regimes after the tested date; and full-sample preprocessing or tuning can leak test information. Use chronological walk-forward splits, purge training labels whose intervals touch test labels, fit transforms only within each training fold, and keep a final untouched holdout. An embargo can add a gap for further dependence, chosen from the information horizon.','Why is merely shuffling less aggressively insufficient?','It does not explicitly remove label overlap or enforce a deployment-time information set. Validation must correspond to what was knowable at prediction time.','l14')
q('q28','A fitted scaler leaks quietly','Validation','Medium','researcher',8,'You split chronologically but fit a normalisation transform on the entire series before training. Explain the leakage and give a correct pipeline.',None,['Preprocessing learns parameters too.','The test distribution has influenced the training representation.'],'The full-series mean and scale incorporate future test observations. Fit each transform on training data, apply it unchanged to validation/test, and repeat inside every model-selection fold. After selection, refit on permitted pre-holdout data only.','Is using a rolling normaliser always safe?','Only if each value uses data available then, with an appropriate shift. A centred window or same-time data unavailable at execution leaks.','l14')
q('q29','Minimum-variance combination','Linear algebra','Medium','researcher',12,'Two unbiased independent estimates of the same mean have variances 1 and 4. Combine them as wX+(1−w)Y. What weight w minimises variance?',0.8,['Minimise w²+4(1−w)².','Differentiate and solve.'],'The derivative is 2w−8(1−w)=0, yielding w=4/5. The resulting variance is 0.8. Greater weight goes to the more precise estimator. Independence and common target are required.','With covariance c, derive the weight.','w=(v_Y−c)/(v_X+v_Y−2c), provided the denominator is positive. The optimum can lie outside [0,1] if unrestricted weights are allowed.','l10')
q('q30','Ridge changes the geometry','Regression','Hard','researcher',12,'For objective ||y−Xβ||²+λ||β||² with λ>0, derive β̂. Explain what happens along a nearly singular direction of X.',None,['Differentiate the quadratic objective.','Diagonalise XᵀX.'],'The normal equation is (XᵀX+λI)β̂=Xᵀy, so β̂=(XᵀX+λI)⁻¹Xᵀy. Along an eigenvector with eigenvalue d, the OLS coefficient is multiplied by d/(d+λ) when OLS is defined. Small-eigenvalue directions shrink most. Usually an intercept is excluded from the penalty.','Why standardise features before ridge?','The penalty is scale-dependent. A change of units changes relative shrinkage unless the feature scale or penalty is adjusted.','l11')
q('q31','A Sharpe ratio needs dependence assumptions','Time series','Hard','researcher',12,'Someone annualises a daily Sharpe by multiplying by √252. Derive the rule for iid daily returns and explain why it may fail with autocorrelation.',None,['Compute the mean and variance of a 252-day sum.','Include all cross-covariances in the dependent case.'],'For iid excess returns with mean μ and variance σ², a k-day sum has mean kμ and variance kσ², giving √k μ/σ. With autocovariance γ_h, variance is kγ₀+2Σ_(h=1)^(k−1)(k−h)γ_h. Positive autocorrelation increases this denominator relative to the iid rule. The derivation uses summed returns; compounding and costs introduce additional distinctions.','Can a smooth equity curve be evidence against a strategy?','It can signal stale pricing or smoothing that hides dependence and risk. Investigate the construction before trusting a high annualised Sharpe.','l13')
q('q32','Fit the best of a thousand','Validation','Hard','researcher',12,'You choose the best of 1,000 models using one validation set, then report that model’s validation score as expected future performance. What is wrong, and how would you fix it?',None,['The validation set has become part of the fitting procedure.','Distinguish selection from estimation.'],'Selection favours models with positive validation noise. The winning score is optimistically biased even if every candidate was fitted only on training data. Use nested time-aware validation for selection and an untouched final test for estimation; record all trials and uncertainty. Retesting repeatedly on the final test turns it into another validation set.','What if the 1,000 models are strongly correlated?','The effective search is smaller than 1,000 independent trials, but selection bias remains. The independence formula cannot be imported without checking dependence.','l15')
q('q33','Predictable is not executable','Research design','Hard','researcher',15,'Your model predicts the next recorded trade price with high accuracy. Design the checks needed before claiming a tradable edge.',None,['A recorded price is not necessarily an executable price.','Define the timestamp and action your strategy could take.','Include spreads, fees, latency, inventory and capacity.'],'Specify the prediction timestamp, publication delays and available features. Rebuild labels from executable bid/ask prices after realistic latency. Compare to a persistence baseline. Audit time ordering, stale prices, leakage and survivorship. Include fees, spread crossing, impact, turnover, shorting constraints and capacity. Evaluate chronological holdouts and P&L under an explicit decision rule. Forecast accuracy alone does not establish profit.','Why might a highly accurate model lose money?','Small wins and rare large losses, adverse selection, or transaction costs can dominate. Accuracy ignores payoff sizes and action constraints.','l16')
q('q34','Explain a research failure','Research design','Medium','researcher',10,'A strategy works in one period and fails in the next. Give a structured diagnosis that distinguishes implementation error, overfitting, regime change and costs.',None,['Start with invariants and a minimal baseline.','Compare signal quality with execution economics separately.'],'First reconcile data, timestamps, universe, labels and positions, and reproduce the earlier period. Then audit trials, tuning and overlap for overfit. Evaluate feature/return distributions and subperiod stability for a regime change. Finally decompose gross versus net returns, turnover, spread, fees and impact. Multiple causes can coexist; a story alone does not identify one.','What evidence would falsify your preferred explanation?','State a predicted diagnostic before running it: for example, a cost explanation predicts persistent gross edge but net erosion that tracks spreads or turnover.','l16')
q('q35','Three rolls and a quoted market','Expected value','Easy','trader',5,'A contract pays the sum of three independent fair dice. A dealer quotes bid 10 and ask 11. Ignoring costs and risk aversion, what is your fair value?',10.5,['Use linearity of expectation.','Each die has mean 3.5.'],'The fair value is 3×3.5=10.5. Buying at 11 or selling at 10 each has expected loss 0.5 per unit, so there is no positive expected-value trade for an uninformed risk-neutral taker.','One die is revealed as 6. What is fair value now?','6+2×3.5=13. Buying at an unchanged ask of 11 has expected edge 2 per unit, before costs and constraints.','l19')
q('q36','The counterparty knows more','Market making','Hard','trader',12,'A contract pays either 0 or 100 with equal prior probabilities. Your ask is 55. Independently of the payoff, a buyer is equally likely to be informed (buys only when payoff=100) or uninformed (always buys). Conditional on a buy, what is the expected payoff?',200/3,['Condition on who buys and the outcome.','A buy occurs with probability 1/4 from informed and 1/2 from uninformed.'],'The event buy has mass 3/4. The joint mass buy and high payoff is 1/4+1/4=1/2. Thus P(high | buy)=2/3 and conditional fair value=200/3. Selling at 55 loses expected value despite being above the prior mean 50.','At what ask do you break even on buys under this fixed behavioural model?','At 200/3. This assumes uninformed buyers buy regardless of the ask and informed buyers still buy in the high state; in realistic models behaviour depends on price.','l20')
q('q37','A risk budget for a repeated bet','Decision theory','Hard','trader',12,'A repeated independent bet wins even money with probability 0.6. You stake fraction f of wealth each time. What f maximises expected log wealth growth?',0.2,['Maximise 0.6 log(1+f)+0.4 log(1−f).','Set the derivative to zero.'],'The derivative is 0.6/(1+f)−0.4/(1−f). Setting it to zero gives f=0.2. The optimum depends on accurate probabilities and the repeated log-utility objective; it is not a universally appropriate real-money stake.','How does uncertainty in p change the decision?','Plug-in Kelly can overbet. Shrinking probabilities toward 1/2 or using a fraction of the estimated stake reduces sensitivity; dependence and tail losses require a different model.','l21')
q('q38','Do not double-count diversification','Risk','Medium','trader',8,'Two returns each have variance 4 and correlation 0.5. What is the variance of an equal-weight portfolio?',3,['Covariance equals correlation times both standard deviations.','Use both off-diagonal terms.'],'Cov=0.5×2×2=2. Portfolio variance=0.25×4+0.25×4+2×0.25×2=3. An average of marginal variances alone would miss diversification; assuming zero covariance would overstate it.','What if the correlation is −1?','The equal-weight portfolio has zero variance under the exact equal-volatility model. That perfect cancellation is fragile to estimated correlations and differing exposures.','l22')
q('q39','Gamma is not delta','Mathematical finance','Medium','trader',8,'A portfolio has delta 0 and gamma 0.08. Under a small underlying move ΔS=5, ignoring time decay and higher-order terms, what is its approximate value change?',1,['Use the second-order Taylor term.','The gamma contribution has a factor of 1/2.'],'ΔV≈delta×ΔS+(1/2)gamma×(ΔS)²=0+0.5×0.08×25=1. Delta neutrality removes the first-order term locally, not all risk.','What happens for ΔS=−5?','The same gamma contribution +1, within the approximation. Theta, volatility changes and rehedging costs are not included.','l23')
q('q40','A synthetic forward','Mathematical finance','Medium','trader',8,'For European options on a non-dividend asset, same strike K=100 and maturity T=1, S=100 and continuously compounded rate r=0. What is C−P?',0,['Use put-call parity.','C−P=S−K exp(−rT).'],'Put-call parity gives C−P=100−100=0. A call minus a put has terminal payoff S_T−K, equal to a forward payoff. European exercise and identical strike/maturity matter.','What changes with known dividend present value D?','C−P=S−D−K exp(−rT). American exercise can prevent equality.','l23')
q('q41','A fair-looking auction','Decision theory','Hard','trader',12,'You bid for an asset with unknown common value. Your estimate is unbiased before bidding. Why can winning make your estimate too high, and how should that enter your bid?',None,['Winning is a selection event.','The highest noisy estimate tends to have positive error.'],'In a common-value auction, winning usually means your estimate exceeded others. Conditional on winning, your estimation error can be positive even when the unconditional error has mean zero. Bid using E[value | your signal, winning] and the auction/payment rules, not the raw estimate. The adjustment depends on competitors and information structure.','Does this apply identically to private-value auctions?','No. If your own consumption value is private and known, rivals’ lower bids need not be bad news about that value. Strategic bid shading can still arise for other reasons.','l24')
q('q42','Two constraints, one quote','Market making','Hard','trader',12,'Your inventory is already long. You can lower both your bid and ask by the same amount or widen your spread symmetrically. Explain how these actions differ, and what information you need to choose.',None,['A skew changes the quote centre.','A wider spread changes execution probabilities on both sides.'],'Lowering the centre encourages selling and discourages further buying, helping reduce long inventory. Symmetric widening reduces fills on both sides and may protect against information risk but need not resolve the position. Choose using fair value, inventory risk, flow sensitivity, adverse selection, horizon and limits. Exact optimal prices require a model.','Why can a profitable expected edge still violate a sensible policy?','Position limits, correlated exposures, capital, tail risk or liquidity can make the trade infeasible even with positive standalone expectation.','l20')
q('q43','An antithetic pair','Simulation','Hard','researcher',12,'For U~Uniform(0,1), estimate E[U²] using the average of U² and (1−U)². Derive whether its variance is smaller than the average of two independent squared uniforms.',None,['Compute Cov(U²,(1−U)²).','E[U²(1−U)²]=1/30 and E[U²]=1/3.'],'Var(U²)=1/5−1/9=4/45. The covariance is 1/30−1/9=−7/90. Pair-average variance is (1/2)(4/45−7/90)=1/180. The average of two independent U² values has variance 2/45. The antithetic pair is unbiased and reduces variance eightfold.','Does antithetic sampling always reduce variance?','No. It needs a favourable covariance structure. For a function symmetric about 1/2, the paired observations are identical: their average has the variance of one draw, twice the variance of two independent draws.','l17')
q('q44','When a sample was available','Research coding','Medium','researcher',9,'A feature is labelled with event time 10:00:00 but arrives at 10:00:02. A simulated trade acts at 10:00:01. Is joining the feature by event time valid? Design the join.',None,['Event time and availability time are different.','Your information set must respect receipt/publication delay.'],'It is invalid if the feature was unavailable until 10:00:02. Join on the most recent feature whose availability time is at or before the decision time, respecting any execution delay. Keep both event and availability timestamps and define tie rules. An as-of join on event time alone can manufacture a predictive edge.','When is an event-time join sufficient?','Only when event time also represents actual availability at the decision point, or a correctly modelled delay is applied. Document that assumption.','l08')
q('q45','The denominator is part of the answer','Statistics','Easy','shared',4,'A variable has variance 9. What is the variance after multiplying it by 2 and adding 7?',36,['Translations do not affect variance.','Scaling multiplies variance by the square of the scale.'],'Var(2X+7)=4Var(X)=36. The standard deviation doubles; the variance quadruples.','What is Corr(X,−2X+7) when Var(X)>0?','It is −1. Linear translation preserves covariance and negative scaling reverses its sign.','l07')
q('q46','A biased coin, twice','Estimation','Easy','shared',5,'A Bernoulli success probability p has a Beta(2,2) prior. Observe 3 successes and 1 failure. What is the posterior mean of p?',5/8,['Add successes and failures to the Beta parameters.','The posterior is Beta(5,3).'],'Conjugacy gives Beta(2+3,2+1)=Beta(5,3), with mean 5/8. The empirical rate 3/4 and posterior mean differ because the prior contributes information.','What is the predictive probability of success on the next trial?','It equals the posterior mean 5/8. This uses exchangeable Bernoulli observations with a common fixed p.','l09')
q('q47','Stop when you look good','Inference','Hard','researcher',12,'You repeatedly compute an ordinary fixed-sample 5% significance test and stop the first time it rejects. Why is the claimed 5% error rate invalid? Give two principled alternatives.',None,['The sampling and stopping plan is part of the test.','Repeated opportunities to reject inflate the chance of crossing.'],'A fixed-sample p-value is calibrated for its specified sample size and procedure. Repeated looks with a data-dependent stop change that procedure and can inflate type-I error. Precommit to the sample size and test, or use a valid sequential procedure such as alpha spending or an appropriate always-valid test. Do not claim every stopping rule is invalid; the test must match it.','Does a martingale automatically justify optional stopping?','No. Optional-stopping equalities require conditions such as bounded stopping times or suitable integrability. Ignoring those conditions causes classic paradoxes.','l12')
q('q48','Choose a control variate','Simulation','Hard','researcher',14,'To estimate E[Y], you use Z=Y−c(X−E[X]) where E[X] is known. Derive the variance-minimising c and the residual variance, assuming Var(X)>0.',None,['Expand Var(Y−cX).','Differentiate the quadratic in c.'],'Var(Z)=Var(Y)+c²Var(X)−2cCov(X,Y). The optimum is c*=Cov(X,Y)/Var(X). Substitution gives Var(Y)(1−Corr(X,Y)²). The estimator remains unbiased for fixed c; estimating c on the same sample can require care in a finite-sample unbiasedness claim.','How would you estimate c without that complication?','Estimate c on an independent pilot sample, then hold it fixed on the main Monte Carlo sample.','l17')

L=[]
def lesson(id,title,topic,path,minutes,intro,sections,example,check,options,correct,explain,qs,codes=None,objectives=None,trainers=None):
    L.append(dict(id=id,title=title,topic=topic,path=path,minutes=minutes,intro=intro,sections=[dict(title=a,text=b) for a,b in sections],example=example,check=check,options=options,correct=correct,explanation=explain,questions=qs,coding=codes or [],objectives=objectives or [],trainers=trainers or []))
lesson(
    'l01', 'Count the right sample space', 'Probability', 'shared', 15,
    'Before calculating a probability, describe the experiment, what counts as one outcome, and why the outcomes have the probabilities you assign to them.',
    [
        ('Start with the experiment, outcomes and event', r'''An experiment is the random procedure you carry out: for example, rolling one die or drawing two balls from a bag. An outcome records one possible result of that procedure. You must decide exactly what information the record contains.

The sample space, written \(\Omega\) and read “omega”, is the set of all possible outcomes. An event, written \(A\), is a collection of outcomes that satisfy a condition you care about.

For one fair six-sided die, \(\Omega=\{1,2,3,4,5,6\}\). If the event is “roll an even number”, then \(A=\{2,4,6\}\). The notation \(|\Omega|\) means the number of outcomes in the sample space; \(|A|\) means the number of outcomes in the event. Here these counts are 6 and 3. The vertical bars mean “number of elements”, not absolute value, when their contents are a set.'''),
        ('When counting gives a probability', r'''If a finite sample space has equally likely outcomes, every outcome has probability \(1/|\Omega|\). Therefore,

\[P(A)=\frac{|A|}{|\Omega|}.\]

Read this as “the probability of the event equals the number of favourable outcomes divided by the total number of equally likely outcomes”. For the even-number event above, \(P(A)=3/6=1/2\).

“Uniformly chosen” means that each outcome in the specified set has the same probability. It is an assumption about the sampling procedure, not a consequence of merely listing outcomes. A biased coin still has two possible outcomes, heads and tails, but counting one favourable outcome out of two does not establish a probability of one half. When outcomes are not equally likely, add their actual probabilities instead.'''),
        ('Two dice: why the outcomes are ordered', r'''Roll two independent fair six-sided dice, one red and one blue. Record an outcome as (red die value, blue die value). The outcome (2,5) differs from (5,2), because the values are assigned to different dice.

There are 6 choices for the red die and, for each choice, 6 choices for the blue die. Thus there are \(6\times6=36\) ordered outcomes. Fairness and independence make each ordered outcome have probability \((1/6)(1/6)=1/36\).

If you instead record only the sum, the possible sums are 2 through 12, but those eleven outcomes are not equally likely. Sum 2 comes only from (1,1), while sum 7 comes from six ordered outcomes. So \(P(\text{sum}=2)=1/36\) and \(P(\text{sum}=7)=6/36\), not one eleventh each. Your choice of sample space must preserve the probabilities.'''),
        ('Drawing two balls: define n before counting', r'''Suppose a bag contains \(n\) individually distinguishable balls, where \(n\geq2\). Draw one ball uniformly, keep it out of the bag, then draw a second ball uniformly from the remaining balls. This is sampling without replacement: the same physical ball cannot appear twice.

Now ignore the order in which the two balls were drawn. An unordered pair records which two balls you obtained; drawing A then B and drawing B then A produce the same pair, AB.

There are \(n\) choices for the first ball and \(n-1\) choices for the second, giving \(n(n-1)\) ordered selections. Every unordered pair is counted exactly twice in that total, once in each order. Divide by two:

\[\binom{n}{2}=\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\]

The notation \(\binom{n}{2}\), read “n choose two”, counts the ways to select two different objects from n objects while ignoring order. For four balls labelled A, B, C and D, the six unordered pairs are AB, AC, AD, BC, BD and CD. The formula gives \(\binom{4}{2}=4\times3/2=6\).

Under the sampling procedure stated above, every unordered pair has the same probability: its two ordered selections each have probability \(1/[n(n-1)]\). Balls of the same colour are still different physical balls. Recording only the colours would group together different numbers of these equally likely pairs.'''),
        ('Replacement changes the experiment', r'''With replacement, you return the first ball before the second draw. Without replacement, you leave it out. These are different experiments and can give different probabilities for the same verbal event.

Suppose the bag contains 6 red balls and 4 blue balls, and the event is “the two colours differ”. Without replacement, a red-first draw leaves 4 blue balls among 9 remaining balls. A blue-first draw leaves 6 red balls among 9 remaining balls. The two possible colour orders cannot both occur, so add their probabilities:

\[P(\text{different colours})=\frac6{10}\frac49+\frac4{10}\frac69=\frac8{15}.\]

With replacement, the second draw again uses the original bag of 10 balls:

\[P(\text{different colours})=\frac6{10}\frac4{10}+\frac4{10}\frac6{10}=\frac{12}{25}.\]

The second denominator is 9 in the first experiment and 10 in the second. State the replacement rule before choosing a counting formula.'''),
        ('Complements and overlapping events', r'''The complement of an event A, written \(A^c\), contains all outcomes in which A does not happen. Since exactly one of A and its complement happens, \(P(A)=1-P(A^c)\).

For two independent fair coin flips, the equally likely ordered outcomes are HH, HT, TH and TT, where H means heads and T means tails. “At least one head” includes HH, HT and TH. Its complement is “no heads”, which contains only TT. The probability is therefore \(1-1/4=3/4\). Listing the favourable outcomes gives the same result, \(3/4\).

For two events A and B, \(A\cup B\) means “A or B, including both”, and \(A\cap B\) means “both A and B”. The addition rule is

\[P(A\cup B)=P(A)+P(B)-P(A\cap B).\]

Adding the first two probabilities counts the overlap twice; subtracting it once corrects this. With the two dice above, let A mean “the red die is six” and B mean “the blue die is six”. Each event has probability \(1/6\); both happen with probability \(1/36\). Thus the chance of at least one six is \(1/6+1/6-1/36=11/36\).'''),
        ('Independence and disjointness are different', r'''Events are independent when learning that one happened does not change the probability of the other. Their joint probability then satisfies \(P(A\cap B)=P(A)P(B)\). Fairness describes one die's probabilities; independence describes how the dice relate to one another. Neither assumption replaces the other.

Events are disjoint when they cannot happen together, so \(P(A\cap B)=0\). For disjoint events, the addition rule reduces to \(P(A\cup B)=P(A)+P(B)\).

For one die, “roll 1” and “roll 2” are disjoint. They are not independent: learning that the die rolled 1 makes the probability of rolling 2 zero. For two independent dice, “red die is six” and “blue die is six” are independent, but they are not disjoint because (6,6) satisfies both.

Before solving an interview problem, write down the experiment, the outcome you record, whether order matters, whether replacement is allowed, and the justification for equal likelihood or independence. Then identify the event and count or add probabilities.'''),
    ],
    r'''Work through the 6-red, 4-blue example using unordered pairs. There are 10 individually distinguishable balls in total, so \(n=10\). Draw two uniformly without replacement and ignore their order.

The total number of equally likely pairs is \(\binom{10}{2}=10\times9/2=45\). To obtain different colours, choose one of the 6 red balls and one of the 4 blue balls. This produces \(6\times4=24\) favourable unordered pairs; each red-blue pair is counted once. Therefore,

\[P(\text{different colours})=\frac{24}{45}=\frac8{15}.\]

This agrees with the red-then-blue plus blue-then-red calculation above. Both methods describe the same experiment. Counting only “red-red, red-blue, blue-blue” and assigning each probability one third would be wrong: those three colour categories contain different numbers of physical pairs.''',
    'For two independent fair coins, what is P(at least one H)?',
    ['1/2','3/4','1','1/4'],1,
    'The four equally likely ordered outcomes are HH, HT, TH and TT. Only TT has no heads. The probability of at least one head is 1 minus 1/4, which is 3/4.',
    ['q01','q05'],['c01']
)
